fr:mainmatter.fr:p:
We'll start this discussion is with proposition and proof in a purely mathematical context;
    from there, we can see the nuances of specification as we move into software at higher and higher levels of abstraction and involvement in daily, human life.
    My goal here is to explore the process of forming software specifications, arguing that rigorous software specification gets harder as the purpose of the software moves from mathematical computation to playing social, political, and economic roles in the world.

Most fundamentally, a mathematician's job is to bring in novel and useful abstract concepts, capture them in their appropriate definitions or conjectures, and place them appropriately in the corpus of mathematical theory.
    Philosophers do this, often by inventing new technical terminology;
    scientists do this by defining new methods and measurements;
    artists do this but instead of technical terms and symbols, they use an artistic medium.

